 ## Overview
-Dual Marching Squares (DMS) is a 2D surface extraction method that adaptively samples a scalar field using a quadtree, placing a single vertex per leaf node at the dual grid position (the center of the leaf cell) to approximate the isosurface [S1, unofficial]. Unlike standard [Marching Squares](918-marching-squares.md), which places vertices on cell edges, DMS simplifies topology by using one vertex per region but does not preserve sharp features because dual vertices remain in cell centers rather than being projected onto the function's features [S1, unofficial].
+Dual Marching Squares (DMS) is a 2D surface extraction algorithm that generates a polygonal mesh from a scalar field by placing a single vertex per grid cell (or quadtree leaf) at the cell's center (dual grid position) and connecting these vertices into quads [S1, S2, unofficial]. Unlike standard Marching Squares, which places vertices on cell edges, DMS produces smoother results and avoids topological inconsistencies and self-intersections common in other techniques, though it may lose sharp features due to the fixed center placement [S1, S2, unofficial]. It builds on [Marching Squares](918-marching-squares.md) by replacing edge-based vertex placement with a dual-grid approach.
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs and outputs:**
-- Input: A 2D scalar field $f(x,y)$, an isovalue threshold $T$, a minimum leaf size $min\_size$, a grid scaling factor $grid\_size$, and a splitting heuristic constant $minSplitDistanceDiagonalFactor$ (default 1000.0) [S1, unofficial].
-- Output: A list of vertices (position, normal, color) and a list of quad indices forming the mesh [S1, unofficial].
-
